# A thirteen-hundred operation clone

Source `3352` is `x ◇ y = y ◇ (y ◇ (x ◇ x))`, and four of its targets -- `335`, `3558`, `3748` and
`4290` -- are settled by one magma and nothing smaller: `Magma.bigCert1` below, a model of `3352`
on `Fin 6` whose binary clone closes at `1304` operations, none of which satisfies any of the four.

Nothing cheaper works.  Every model of `3352` on four points or fewer is known exhaustively and
none of them refutes anything; the magma's proper subalgebras are `{0, 2}`, `{1, 3}`, `{4, 5}`,
`{0, 2, 4, 5}` and `{1, 3, 4, 5}`, with clones of `10`, `10`, `10`, `13` and `13`, and every one of
those clones does contain a witness for all four targets.  So the whole clone is needed, and at
`1304` members it is far past what `![...]` can carry: the tables are ASCII strings instead, read
back through `ByteArray`, which indexes in constant time.

`Magma.bigCert1Data` holds the clone as `1304` blocks of `36` digits `0`-`5`, and
`Magma.bigCert1Comp` holds each composite index as two characters of base `40`, offset by `'0'` --
base `32` as in `Definability.Clone870` would only reach `1024`.  The `1304 * 1304 * 36` equations
of `Magma.IsCloneFamily.comp` are checked by `native_decide`, so the four refutations below
additionally depend on `Lean.ofReduceBool`.  Each `native_decide` recompiles the module and the
module is megabytes of string, so the calls are batched into two: one conjunction for the clone
family and one for the four refutations.

This file is generated by `genbig.py`.
-/

